"""Authored narration paired with real application captures, not fabricated screens."""
SCENES=[
('Welcome to GlucoTwin','overview', 'Open the Executive Overview.', '''Welcome to GlucoTwin, a research prototype for a continuously updating patient digital twin. This walkthrough uses actual captures from the running local application, with synthesized narration and deliberate pauses to inspect the screen. It is edited, rather than an uninterrupted recording of a live patient. Every patient, laboratory value, sensor trace and event in this project is synthetic. The system does not diagnose diabetes, recommend treatment, or demonstrate improved clinical outcomes. The engineering goal is to combine historical patient context with changing sensor observations, then estimate whether a defined glucose event will occur within two hours. We will inspect the patient profile, replay controls, evolving state, model explanations, scenario inputs, held-out evaluation, and audit log. The source package also contains the trained model, generator, tests, architecture and presentation.'''),
('The healthcare question','overview','Point to the event definition and horizon.', '''The problem motivating this prototype is the difference between occasional historical measurements and an ongoing physiological trajectory. A laboratory measurement can describe a patient's baseline, while a sensor stream describes recent movement. GlucoTwin joins those two views for a narrowly defined research task. At a prediction timestamp, it asks whether any simulated glucose reading will exceed one hundred and eighty milligrams per deciliter during the next one hundred and twenty minutes. A high reading is an event definition, not a diagnosis. The dashboard shows the forecast horizon alongside the latest available observation. Its percentage is an estimate learned from synthetic data. It requires interpretation and has no validated clinical uncertainty interval. Notice that the screen labels the selected patient, simulation time and synthetic data status instead of implying a real wearable connection.'''),
('Historical patient context','profile','Inspect the EHR-like record.', '''The Patient Profile page shows the historical stream. Each fictional identifier has its own age, descriptive sex category, diabetes duration, historical fasting glucose, HbA1c and baseline glucose statistics. Diagnosis and medication category fields provide context, but they are fictional and medication effects are not modeled. The historical baseline is generated before the sensor stream. It is not calculated by averaging the full future timeline, which would leak information into a supposedly early prediction. Several historical fields enter the trained classifier. Other fields remain descriptive. The same patient identifier links the profile to a separate compressed sensor file. Changing the dropdown loads that patient's own data. This persistent identity is the foundation for a patient-conditioned twin, although the predictor remains a shared population model rather than an individually fitted physiological model.'''),
('Synthetic population design','profile','Explain the generator and its assumptions.', '''The bundled experiment contains one hundred and sixty fictional patients with seven days of five-minute sensor observations each. That gives three hundred and twenty-two thousand five hundred and sixty sensor rows. The simulator creates patient-specific baselines and different meal-response strengths. Meals occur at jittered times, and a delayed response kernel raises glucose afterward. Activity, circadian rhythm, prior completed sleep and autocorrelated noise also affect the trace. Heart rate changes with activity. Some glucose values are randomly missing, and each patient has an additional contiguous sensor gap. These relationships are programmed assumptions, not discoveries about human physiology. The model learns patterns in this generated world. Strong performance therefore demonstrates that the pipeline can learn the simulator, rather than establishing that its predictions will work for real patients. The fixed seed makes the experiment repeatable.'''),
('A functioning replay engine','live_initial','Open Live Digital Twin and inspect initial state.', '''The Live Digital Twin page is the central demonstration. It begins after an hour of synthetic observations so trailing features have a warmup period. The plot contains only observations up to the current replay cursor. Start or Resume begins deterministic replay, Pause stops advancement, Step advances one five-minute observation, and Advance moves one hour. Reset returns this patient's replay to the initial position. These are simulated-time controls, not a claim of real-time wearable ingestion. The tinted area to the right marks the two-hour classification horizon. It is intentionally empty because this implementation predicts an event probability, not a future glucose curve. We avoid drawing a plausible-looking forecast that was never trained or evaluated. The timestamp and quality indicator identify exactly which state the displayed estimate describes.'''),
('Start, pause and resume','live_running','Start playback, observe timestamp changes, then pause.', '''In this captured state, playback has advanced the sensor cursor. Each new arrival passes the observed prefix through the shared feature builder and then invokes the trained estimator. The result is not a manually assigned score or a fixed number that simply redraws. The application uses a timed Streamlit fragment to advance while playback is active. Pause preserves the current cursor and state, and Resume continues from that position. The five-minute data interval is compressed into a short demonstration tick, so simulation time moves faster than wall-clock time. That distinction matters when explaining the demo to judges. The current percentage can change modestly or substantially depending on the observed trajectory; neither direction is guaranteed. The audit records the timestamp, observed value, estimate, horizon, review category and model version for each issued state.'''),
('Single-step state updates','live_step','Use Step +5 min and compare the timestamp.', '''Single-step replay is useful for verifying the twin's behavior. One click advances exactly one observation, recomputes trailing statistics and updates the latest estimate. The fifteen-minute slope compares the current causally filled glucose value with the value three slots earlier. The rolling mean and variability summarize the recent hour. Step counts and heart rate summarize recent activity information. The twin also retains historical baseline characteristics for the same patient. This creates a traceable chain from newly available observations to the new model input and output. A test verifies that advancing changes the state timestamp, that repeated updates at the same timestamp do not duplicate the audit row, and that reset restores the cursor. These properties distinguish a stateful monitoring prototype from a static chart with an unchanging risk label.'''),
('A longer trajectory','live_meal','Advance several hours and inspect the observed trace.', '''The hour-advance control makes a longer timeline practical to demonstrate. Here the current view contains several hours of this patient's observed synthetic glucose. The model combines current level with trailing mean, variability, slope, recent steps, heart rate, meal timing and historical context. Future scheduled meals are never passed into the feature vector. Clock features can nevertheless help in this simulator because meals follow habitual times. That is a known source of simulator-specific predictability and a reason to avoid generalizing these scores to clinical populations. The dashed horizontal line is the configured glucose event threshold. Crossing that line at the current time is not itself the future label, because label construction excludes the current reading. The main evaluation does, however, include cases where an existing elevation persists into the future.'''),
('Inspect the digital twin state','state','Expand the state inspector and prediction history.', '''The state inspector makes the internal representation reviewable. It contains the patient identifier, current simulation timestamp, latest actual glucose observation, event probability when available, horizon, review category, model version, data-quality status and engineered features. The session also retains prediction history for the selected patient. Different patients have separate in-memory replay objects, so their histories do not mix. The SQLite log persists across app restarts, while replay cursor state is session-local. Reset clears the current replay history but intentionally preserves previously recorded audit rows. The database key combines patient, timestamp and model version, which prevents duplicate entries when the same state is revisited. This is a lightweight local design suited to a one-day prototype. It is not a production clinical record system and has no authentication or multi-user synchronization layer.'''),
('How features stay causal','features','Inspect the feature table and feature definitions.', '''The feature table is built by the same function used during training. Rolling windows trail the current timestamp; none are centered on future values. A short glucose gap can be forward-filled for at most three five-minute slots. There is no backward fill from a future reading. Missingness and elapsed time since the last actual glucose value remain explicit features. Historical normalization subtracts the pre-stream baseline, while the expanding normalization uses only the observed past. The estimator receives an enumerated list of twenty-two columns. Patient identifiers, future glucose, labels and hidden simulator response parameters are excluded. The automated temporal isolation test replaces all later sensor values and confirms that earlier feature rows remain identical. This is a direct check of a central scientific requirement: unavailable future observations must not influence the live estimate.'''),
('Interpreting model sensitivity','explain','Read the sensitivity plot and its limitations.', '''The explainability page uses an intentionally simple local sensitivity method. It starts from this patient's current feature vector, replaces one feature with its training-set median, and asks how the model estimate changes. The chart displays the original probability minus the replacement probability. A positive value means the original feature setting produced a higher estimate than that particular replacement. These values are not SHAP values, do not add up to the model output, and do not establish clinical causes. Correlated features can produce unrealistic combinations when only one column is changed. For example, replacing the current glucose while leaving related rolling features unchanged is a model probe rather than a physiological experiment. The interface makes these limitations explicit so a viewer can inspect associations without mistaking them for confirmed mechanisms or treatment guidance.'''),
('Scenario: recent glucose trajectory','scenario_glucose','Adjust the recent-trajectory slider.', '''The scenario page explores input sensitivity using a copy of the already observed history. The first scenario gradually changes glucose across the most recent thirty minutes, then recalculates all engineered features before calling the model. This is more coherent than manually changing a displayed probability or editing only a single derived statistic. The original twin state and persistent audit log remain unchanged. The two cards compare the original estimate with the altered-input estimate. Their difference is not a validated counterfactual effect. A changed recent trajectory can fall outside the training distribution, and the model does not prove that a real-world action could create that trajectory. The purpose is to make the learned mapping inspectable for a researcher or judge. The chart below displays the altered observed inputs, not a newly predicted future glucose curve.'''),
('Scenario: a meal annotation','scenario_meal','Choose Meal annotation now.', '''The meal scenario marks an event at the current observed timestamp. That changes the time-since-meal feature after the feature pipeline runs again. It does not add a future glucose rise or use the simulator's hidden meal response to manufacture a favorable prediction. A model estimate may change because meal annotations were associated with future events in training, but the change is still an association inside this synthetic experiment. Meal size, composition and individual absorption dynamics are not available to this classifier. The interface therefore avoids nutritional advice or claims about how a real meal would affect a patient. This scenario also exposes an important modeling limitation: the model may rely on habitual timing and recent glucose rather than respond strongly to a single annotation. A small change is a legitimate result and is not hidden.'''),
('Scenario: recent activity','scenario_activity','Choose Recent activity and vary the added steps.', '''The activity scenario adds a configurable number of steps across the recent thirty-minute prefix and increases the corresponding heart-rate observations. It then rebuilds trailing features and compares the estimate. This is an illustrative input perturbation, not a prescription to exercise and not evidence that activity will lower a particular patient's glucose. The model does not simulate future activity physiology or adjust medication. The scenario intentionally holds other inputs constant, so some combinations can be unlike the generated training data. This limitation belongs in any explanation to a healthcare judging panel. The useful engineering demonstration is that multiple observed inputs can be changed together, the same feature pipeline processes them, and the resulting model output is available for inspection without modifying the original patient replay or persistent prediction record.'''),
('Scenario: missing sensor interval','scenario_missing','Choose Missing sensor interval and observe withholding.', '''This scenario removes the last thirty minutes of glucose observations. The quality gate checks how long it has been since an actual reading and the fraction of missing values in the recent hour. When those limits are exceeded, GlucoTwin withholds the estimate instead of presenting a confident-looking number. The latest actual measurement is also labeled missing. Shorter gaps may permit inference using a recent forward-filled value, but the interface still exposes data quality. Missing data therefore affect both the feature representation and whether the model is allowed to produce an estimate. This behavior is covered by an automated test. It is a research safeguard rather than a clinically validated safety rule. The thresholds are implementation choices documented in the methodology, and real-world deployment would require much more extensive missingness and device-failure validation.'''),
('Scenario: patient context','scenario_profile','Choose an alternate historical profile.', '''The profile comparison keeps the observed sensor prefix while substituting another synthetic historical record. This demonstrates how the shared model is conditioned on patient context and baseline normalization. It does not claim that the combined record describes a plausible real person; the mismatch is an explicit sensitivity experiment. Patient-specific features include historical glucose, HbA1c, diabetes duration and deviations from an individual baseline. That is a meaningful form of input personalization, but it does not automatically imply improved predictive accuracy. We tested a dynamic-only comparator that excludes all historical and explicitly personalized features. On this particular seeded experiment, that comparator performed slightly better. The project therefore distinguishes implementing personalization from proving that personalization helps. The next evaluation page reports both results so this limitation is visible rather than buried in documentation.'''),
('Patient-separated model evaluation','performance','Compare fused, rule and dynamic-only metrics.', '''The evaluation uses eighty-eight training patients, twenty-four validation patients, twenty-four calibration patients and twenty-four final test patients. No patient's overlapping time windows are randomly distributed across those partitions. The model selects between two leaf-count settings using validation average precision. Calibration uses separate patients, and the alert cutoff is fixed at one half. On eleven thousand five hundred and sixty-seven held-out windows, the fused model achieved precision about point eight seven three, recall point eight one nine, and F one point eight four five. The simple glucose-plus-positive-trend rule achieved F one point six two seven. These are actual computed results stored in the metrics file, not manually entered dashboard scores. Overlapping windows remain correlated within the test partition, so window count should not be mistaken for the number of independent patient outcomes.'''),
('False positives and early warning','performance','Inspect confusion matrix and below-threshold subset.', '''The fused model's confusion matrix contains six thousand eight hundred and seventy-nine true negatives, five hundred false positives, seven hundred and fifty-nine false negatives, and three thousand four hundred and twenty-nine true positives. Its false-positive rate is about six point eight percent. False-positive windows are not independent alert episodes, and no episode deduplication or cooldown analysis has been implemented. The primary task includes continuation of elevations already present at prediction time. To assess a more relevant early-warning subset, the report separately evaluates windows whose current glucose is at or below the event threshold. That subset has lower F one, about point seven four five, and recall about point seven zero. Reporting the subset prevents the strong overall result from being misrepresented as equally strong advance detection of entirely new excursions.'''),
('Calibration and honest comparison','calibration','Read the reliability diagram and Brier results.', '''The reliability diagram compares average predicted probability with the observed event frequency in synthetic test bins. A diagonal line represents ideal agreement. The model uses sigmoid calibration fitted on a separate patient partition, but fitting a calibrator does not guarantee improved test performance. Here the raw Brier score was approximately point zero six eight six seven, and the calibrated score was point zero six nine five two. Lower is better, so this metric became slightly worse. Brier also reflects discrimination and outcome uncertainty, not calibration alone. The dynamic-only ablation achieved F one about point eight four seven, slightly above the fused model's point eight four five. No statistical significance or superiority claim is made. These comparisons are retained because scientific integrity is more useful than presenting only the favorable baseline comparison.'''),
('Global importance and limitations','importance','Inspect validation permutation importance.', '''Global feature importance uses permutation on validation data, measuring the decrease in average precision when a feature is shuffled. Two repeats and a limited sample size keep the computation practical for the prototype. Correlated predictors can share information, so low permutation importance does not prove a feature is clinically irrelevant. The synthetic environment also embeds repeated meal schedules, baseline differences and specific response equations. A model that exploits those assumptions may fail on real sensor data, different devices or a different population. The project has no external clinical validation, prospective study, subgroup fairness analysis, patient-level uncertainty intervals, or demonstrated health benefit. Those are material limitations, not minor implementation details. The present evidence supports the functioning architecture and reproducible synthetic experiment, while a clinical research program would need independent data and appropriately designed validation.'''),
('Audit trail and retrospective inspection','log','Export the prediction log; explain the retrospective switch.', '''The Prediction Log page reads the persistent SQLite audit for the selected synthetic patient. It records the simulation timestamp, actual observed glucose when present, event estimate when permitted, horizon, review category and model version. A CSV download supports inspection outside the application. Resetting replay does not silently erase this record. The separate retrospective option reveals the complete recorded synthetic timeline only after an explicit user choice and warning. Those future values are ground truth for review, not model forecasts. Keeping retrospective inspection separate from the live page prevents future observations from appearing to be known at prediction time. This separation also makes the demonstration easier to audit: a judge can watch a prediction being issued from an observed prefix, then inspect later data in the clearly labeled retrospective mode.'''),
('Reproduce and submit responsibly','overview','Show setup commands, artifacts and submission checklist.', '''To run the project locally, create a Python virtual environment, install the pinned requirements and start Streamlit with app dot py. The package already includes synthetic sensor files and the trained model. To reproduce training, run the GlucoTwin train module with one hundred and sixty patients, seven days and seed twenty twenty-six, then run the test suite. The tests cover temporal feature isolation, exact target boundaries, missing-data withholding, disjoint patient partitions, replay state and all dashboard pages. The package includes methodology, a data dictionary, model card, MIT license, architecture PDF and presentation PDF. This narrated walkthrough uses edited application captures and synthesized speech; the source script supports a participant-recorded version. Before submission, publish and verify the actual repository and video links, confirm team details, and retain the explicit statement that all performance is synthetic research performance.''')
]
